Our old Shark steam mop died about ten days ago after roughly ten years of service. That's not a typo. Ten years of daily grime, dropped spaghetti, and muddy boot prints, and the thing just kept going. When it finally gave out, it felt less like an appliance breaking and more like losing a member of the cleaning crew. So when it came time to replace it, there wasn't much debate in the OTD household about brand loyalty. The only real question was which Shark?

That question belonged to the Dudess. She's the one who actually runs point on floor cleaning around here, and she's the one whose knees have earned the right to an opinion. She'd heard about the Steam & Scrub with Steam Blaster Technology, looked into it, and made the call. We also picked up the optional scrub brush attachment for deep cleaning, mainly so her knees *(or the only good one I have left)* wouldn't be part of it at all.

**Worth knowing up front:** we don't run this on our engineered hardwood. It's strictly a tile mop in our house, working the bathrooms, kitchen, and entryway. Shark markets this as safe for sealed hardwood, marble, stone, laminate, and vinyl too, using the softer cloth pads that come standard, and that may well be true. We're just not the household to test that particular claim.

**Setup.** It showed up the next day after ordering, packaged well, and went together fast, no manual-wrestling required. The cleaning pads attach to the mop's rotating pad heads with a simple hook-and-loop system, and molded plastic guides keep them centered so they don't wander off-axis as they spin. Shark is firm on one rule: distilled water only, no chemicals in the tank. Follow that instruction and don't improvise.

**Running it.** Getting the steam going and feeling those pads spin up for the first time is honestly a little fun, especially with the scrub brushes attached instead of the standard cloth pads. It's not a fight to control, and it does the job it promises. The scrub brushes get down into grout lines that the old mop could never touch, while the steam itself still does the heavy lifting the same way it always has.

This model also has the Steam Blaster built in, and it's become our go-to move for the one spot that's actually dirty instead of the whole floor. Squeeze the trigger, aim it right at a particular grimy patch, and it hits that one area with a concentrated blast of steam to loosen up dried-on food, grime, or whatever's stuck there, before the rotating pads finish the job. It's a small feature, but a genuinely useful one for kitchen duty specifically.

**Who might like this.** This is built for households with sealed hard flooring that actually takes a beating, kitchens, bathrooms, entryways, anywhere grout lines and stuck-on grime are a recurring fight. If your floors are mostly light-traffic carpet or engineered hardwood, you're protective of, this isn't solving a problem you have. If you've got tile that collects grime in the grout and you're tired of getting on your hands and knees with a brush to clean it, this is exactly the tool for the job.

**The verdict.** It does a genuinely good job of keeping us off our knees for routine cleaning, and it handles the stuck-on stuff that kitchen life throws at a floor. Is it worth the extra money over a basic steam mop? Between the two of us, our knees vote yes without hesitation. The real test now is durability, and that verdict is still a few years out.  But so far, it is looking good.

**Bottom line:** a good recommendation, with one open question on long-term durability that only time will answer.
